{"mode": "replace", "path": "/home/bob/books/First_Generation/Chapter_64_Sol_7305.md", "old_string": "The colony had grown outward from the original landing site, not in a random scatter but in the slow, deliberate radial expansion that engineering logic had dictated and necessity had enforced. From the lander habitats — the three modules that had carried them down through the thin atmosphere and landed with bone-jarring finality on the surface of Mars — the tunnels had spread like roots. First a connecting corridor, because you could not live in separate modules and share resources efficiently. Then a secondary habitation ring, because thirty people needed more room than three modules could provide, especially when those modules had been designed for the brief post-landing period before the first supply ship arrived. Then the greenhouses, because you cannot starve and plan a long-term future simultaneously. Then the workshops, the storage areas, the water reclamation plants, the airlock complexes that allowed controlled access to the surface. Then more habitation, because the births started and the children grew and the space that had been adequate for thirty adults was insufficient for forty-seven adults and fourteen children. Then deeper levels, because the surface radiation demanded it and the thermal stability of the subsurface offered advantages that no one had fully appreciated in the beginning.

And now the outer ring: tunnels being dug by machines that had been built by machines that had been designed by humans who had, themselves, been sustained by systems built by humans who had arrived with very little and built very much. The outer ring was where the new families lived, where the new workshops operated, where the new greenhouses grew not just food but experimental crops — strains of wheat and barley modified for low gravity and filtered light, strains of legumes that had been bred to fix nitrogen in regolith that had been processed and amended and coaxed into something approaching soil. The outer ring was the future, physically and otherwise, and it was being built by people who had never seen Earth and would, in all likelihood, never see Earth, and who were, in fact, exactly where they wanted to be.", "new_string": "The colony had grown outward from the original landing site in the slow, deliberate radial expansion that engineering logic had dictated and necessity had enforced. From the three lander modules, the tunnels had spread like roots: connecting corridors, then a secondary habitation ring, then the greenhouses, then the workshops and storage and water reclamation and airlocks, then deeper levels as the radiation demanded it. Layer upon layer, each one solving a problem the previous layer had created, each one making room for more people than anyone had planned for.

And now the outer ring, where new families lived and new greenhouses grew experimental crops — strains of wheat and legumes bred to fix nitrogen in regolith that had been coaxed into something approaching soil. The outer ring was the future, built by people who had never seen Earth and were, in fact, exactly where they wanted to be."}
